{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkWvwYGne6H7TuGJySqf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Cr1T7XxmZyocwVqkGjz54a","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"The line opens with a double bar. Bar 1 is two quarter rests (drawn as ~ shapes), then a quarter #D marked \"pia.\". That is bar 87: rest, rest, D#3, with \"pia.\" where the edition has pp. Bar 2 is two beamed E 8ths, then an E with a stroke through its stem, the shorthand for repeated 8ths. A slur covers the first notes and there are tremolo-like wavy marks above. That is bar 88's six E3 8ths. Bars 3 to 6 each hold one dotted-half note with a stroke through the stem, so each stands for six repeated 8ths. They step down from D (middle line) to C, B and A, matching bars 89 (D3), 90 (C3), 91 (B2) and 92 (A2). One \"for.\" written above bar 5 is not in the edition. Bar 7 is a half C3 marked \"poco for.\", then a high quarter C4 with a slur starting. That is bar 93. Bar 8 is a high quarter D4 ending the slur, a quarter D3, an 8th rest and an 8th D3 marked \"pia.\". That is bar 94, and the barline at the right edge ends the line. Bar 95's E 8ths are not on this line. There are 8 bars in all: 87 to 94.","right":true,"insights":"The copyist writes the repeated 8ths of bars 88 to 92 as single stroked notes, so those bars must be matched by their falling pitches (E, D, C, B, A), not by counting notes. The sharp before D in bar 87 and the poco f leap to C4 in bar 93 pin down both ends of the line. A note in the edition text saying which bars repeat one pitch would have made the stroked notes quicker to confirm."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5278,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5278},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","infere